<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minab</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minab is a city in Hormozgan Province in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on the Minab River near the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>. It lies near the site of Old Hormuz, the mainland port and capital of the kingdom of Hormuz until around 1300, when raids drove its king to move to the island of Jarun. The area is known for its date palms and its Thursday market.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minab.jpg" alt="Minab" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: نادر مظلومی · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BANDAR_ABBAS_(3)_%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D9%86%D9%85%D8%A7%DB%8C%DB%8C_%D8%A7%D8%B2_%D8%B4%D9%87%D8%B1_%D9%85%DB%8C%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%A8_%D8%AF%D8%B1_%D9%87%D8%B1%D9%85%D8%B2%DA%AF%D8%A7%D9%86.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Minab is a city in the Central District of Minab County in Hormozgan province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and the capital of both the county and the district. It was historically part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>. Its climate is a hot desert one: summer daytime averages reach 37 degrees from May to September, and annual rainfall is around 124 millimetres. Most of its people speak a Bandari dialect of Persian and are Shia Muslims. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 54,623 people in 2006, 63,229 in 2011 and 73,170 in 2016.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human presence around Minab reaches into remote prehistory: Middle Palaeolithic stone tools 200,000 to 250,000 years old were found there in the 1970s, and pottery from the 3rd millennium BCE in a nearby village. Painted pottery from the Parthian and Sasanian periods has also been found. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the area was well populated, as large quantities of East Asian ceramics show, reflecting maritime trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. A fortress at Minab is documented in 1318, when it sheltered the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>. Minab, Manujan and Vashkerd were governed each by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> as the outer territories of the original Kingdom of Hormuz.</p><p>In 1515 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>, which Safavid Iran disputed, since it counted Hormuz as its own vassal. When the Ottoman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piri-reis">Piri Reis</a> attacked Hormuz in 1552, Iranian and Portuguese families took refuge in Minab. On 17 October 1602 Iranian forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allahverdi-khan">Allahverdi Khan</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, attacked Minab and Shamil to stop Portuguese attempts to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>. After a three-month siege of Shamil the army took Minab quickly, with heavy casualties on both sides. In 1621 Maani, the wife of the Italian composer Pietro Della Valle, died there. Later the governor Emamqoli Khan made an alliance at Minab with British East India Company representatives, who agreed to help Abbas the Great expel the Portuguese from Hormuz and Qeshm Island.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> fell in 1722 the mayor, Mir Abdollah, asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> for help in defending the fort, without success. Afghan troops captured Minab in 1727, and in 1744 Mohammad Baqer Beg Lari plundered the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> placed Minab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-abbas">Bandar Abbas</a> under temporary Dutch administration that year. About 1794 the Omani Empire captured Minab and held it through much of the 19th century. A Dutch record of 1756 said its people lived from agriculture, not navigation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the 21st century the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps Navy's Asef Missile Brigade was based at Minab by 2019. On 28 February 2026, during Israeli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> strikes on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, an elementary girls' school in the city was struck, and Iranian officials said at least 180 people, mostly schoolgirls, were killed and 95 injured.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minab” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Arab and Persian traditions say the kingdom was founded in the 11th century on the mainland near modern <b>Minab</b> by a ruler from Oman.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Kingdom of Ormus</a></li></ul></section>
